Title: GREYHARBOR
Episode: 107 "Bell Water"
Credit: Original fixture material written for the Canon AI test harness.

EXT. GREYHARBOR DOCKS - MORNING

The stranded boat sits crooked beyond the shoals. No one admits seeing it arrive.

Mara, Gideon, and Lila watch Hale's men rope off the pier.

GIDEON
State police move like they own the tide.

MARA
Nobody owns it. Some people just rent better lies.

INT. BAIT SHOP - DAY

JORY PIKE, thin as a file blade, sorts hooks behind the counter. Mara is nowhere near the shop.

Edda buys twine and keeps her bandaged hand tucked in her apron.

JORY
Tell Mara the stove pipe was clever.

JORY
But clever burns when the stove gets hot.

Edda stops counting coins.

EDDA
Who told you that?

JORY
Greyharbor tells everybody eventually.

EXT. FOG BELL BUOY - NIGHT

The Fog Bell Buoy rises whole from the water, cage slick with weed, as if it never burned.

Danny clings to the ladder while Gideon steadies the skiff below.

GIDEON
Don't look down.

DANNY
Bad advice for a man over water.

EXT. WATER BELOW THE BUOY - CONTINUOUS

The skiff rope snaps. The current takes it beyond reach.

Danny dives from the buoy and swims hard across the black water toward the skiff.

Gideon stares, stunned.

GIDEON
Danny!

Danny catches the rope and gasps like his ribs are breaking.

INT. EDDA'S SHACK - NIGHT

Mara tears the stove pipe apart. The sleeve is empty.

EDDA
It was there before noon.

MARA
Jory knew before noon.

EDDA
Then Jory is either a thief or a window.

MARA
Windows break.

INT. FERRY OFFICE - NIGHT

Hale spreads photographs across Gideon's desk: the buoy, the stranded boat, Cole's body.

HALE
This town has a habit of losing evidence and finding ghosts.

GIDEON
Ghosts don't pay dock fees.

HALE
No. Living men do, under dead names.

EXT. OLD CHAPEL ROAD - LATE NIGHT

Mara follows blue hooks tied along the brambles until they end at the burned chapel stones.

There, in the ash, someone has written one word in flour:

OSPREY.

FADE OUT.
